# Medical Policy Determination — shared PA determination activities (CANONICAL)
library "Medical Policy Determination".

/*
The SHARED, reusable prior-authorization DETERMINATION activities for every medical-policy coverage
decision, as CONFIGURED-vocabulary dispositions (kit `configure-dispositions`): activity names are
`<category>.<key>` — the category a PAS review-action (certify / not-certify / pended), the key a
deployment reason/flavor. THIS deployment (IEHP) runs `mode: "embedded"`.
All are COMMUNICATED determinations (CPGCommunicationRequest) — the payer communicates a decision; it
NEVER orders the service (never CPGServiceRequest). A policy references the ones it uses and declares
the matching keys in its package.json `crl.dispositions.options`.

This file is CANONICAL; the copy vendored into each artifact's src/crl is a mirror — edit HERE and re-vendor.

VENDORED FOR MCPM BLEPHAROPLASTY: trimmed to EXACTLY the activities this policy's decision
recommends (kit EMIT-hygiene / CRL #216 — a declared-but-never-recommended activity emits an
ORPHANED AD/PD that advertises a determination the policy can never produce). That is TWO:
  • certify.Met        — medical-policy criteria met (certify, PAS review-action A1).
  • not-certify.Unmet  — criteria not met (not-certify, A3).

⚠⚠ `not-certify.Cosmetic` WAS VENDORED HERE AND HAS BEEN REMOVED — SETTLED BY THE CUSTOMER.
An earlier vendoring carried a third activity for the benefit exclusion, on the KE argument that
"not a benefit when performed for cosmetic purposes" is a different KIND of denial from "not
medically necessary" (a request can fail the criteria without being cosmetic, and a cosmetic request
can carry documentation satisfying an indication, so the two are not complements). Both KE reviewers
reached that independently. It was still WRONG for this deployment: review flag 21dc988d, authored by
a human reviewer, rules "Cosmetic should be unmet". The authority on a coverage-changing question is
the customer's ruling, not the encoder's parse, so the set is 2-way and the cosmetic exclusion keeps
its own FIRST-evaluated branch (it short-circuits the medical criteria) while reporting Unmet. The
reasoning above is kept because it explains what the encoding gives up — a reviewer can no longer
tell a cosmetic denial from a criteria denial by disposition alone — not to re-litigate the ruling.

The disposition SET is read per-policy from the source and the customer's rulings, never inherited
from a sibling artifact.
*/

activity "certify.Met":
- request CPGCommunicationRequest.
- with `Medical-policy criteria MET — the requested service meets this policy's coverage criteria. A communicated sub-determination (embedded mode: feeds a larger cross-company adjudication that issues the actual authorization); certify category / PAS review-action A1; NOT a service order.`.

activity "not-certify.Unmet":
- request CPGCommunicationRequest.
- with `Medical-policy criteria NOT MET — the requested service does not meet this policy's coverage criteria. A communicated sub-determination (embedded mode); not-certify category / PAS review-action A3; NOT a service order.`.
